\section{The \code{youngFilter} argument}{
The module applied the species filter to the mature and immature classes when
\code{!all(pixelValue \%in\% vegTypeIDs)}, but to the young class when
\code{all(pixelValue \%in\% vegTypeIDs)} -- the opposite condition. The git history shows
this is an unfinished copy-paste: the line was introduced (\code{ad5ddb3}) as a copy of
the mature guard that still read \code{sppEquiv["mature"]} in its condition while
assigning to the young class, and the follow-up (\code{b6d600c}) repaired the subset key
but not the dropped \code{!}.

Restoring the \code{!} is nonetheless \strong{not} the fix. Because every \code{pixelValue} in the
lookup comes from the attribute table, the condition is only ever asking whether the
class has any rows at all, so the correct behaviour -- for all three classes -- is to
apply the filter unconditionally. That is what \code{"always"} does.

\code{"legacy"} and \code{"always"} agree except in one case: when no vegetation type present
in the landscape has a \code{"young"}-classified age row. Then \code{"legacy"} skips the filter,
every young pixel is assigned \code{NA} from an empty lookup, and that \code{NA} overwrites the
immature rate the pixel had already been given -- so young stands silently fall
through to \code{ROSother} instead of their \code{immature_young} rate. With LandMine's default
\code{ROSTable}, \code{"young"} appears only for pine, so this bites on any landscape with no
pine in it, and not otherwise.

The default is \code{"legacy"} so that promoting this function out of the module changed
no results.
}

\section{Attribute-table row order}{
The lookup is built from \code{which()} and \code{grep()} over the attribute table, which give
row \emph{positions}; those are translated into raster \emph{values} via the table's own value
column. Row position and raster value coincide as \code{LandR::vegTypeMapGenerator} builds
the map, but not necessarily after a \code{terra::writeRaster()}/\code{terra::rast()} round
trip, which may return the rows in a different order. Code that reloads a saved
\code{vegTypeMap} and indexes by position -- as this function's predecessor in the LandMine
module did -- assigns whole species the wrong fuel type with no error. Measured on a
saved WesternAlbertaUpland \code{vegTypeMap}, that meant aspen and pine, 36\% of the
landscape between them, taking each other's and softwood's rates of spread.
}

\section{Mixedwood stands never use the table's mixed rates}{
\code{knownSpecies} maps species onto \code{spruce}, \code{pine}, \code{decid} and \code{softwood} only.
The fifth fuel type, \code{mixed}, is invented for attribute-table entries that match
no species, so it exists on one side of the species join and not the other: the
join leaves its \code{leading} value \code{NA}, and the subsequent join onto \code{ROSTable}
(which is keyed on \code{leading}) drops it. Mixedwood pixels therefore never receive
the \code{ROSTable}'s \code{mixed} rates at any age, and instead fall through to \code{ROSother}.

With LandMine's default table this means mixedwood burns at 30 rather than 12
(young/immature) or 17 (mature) -- i.e. at the mature-spruce rate -- and both
\code{mixed} rows of the table are dead entries. This is preserved here rather than
fixed, because changing it changes the fire regime; a test pins the behaviour so
that any future change is deliberate.
}

